>
  <h2>What tempo means</h2>
  <p>Tempo is how long each part of a rep takes. On the bench, that is the lowering phase (the eccentric), any pause on the chest, and the press. "Controlling the eccentric" means lowering the bar on purpose instead of letting it drop. A slow eccentric might be 3 or 4 seconds down. A pause means the bar sits on your chest, without bouncing, for a count before you press.</p>

  <h2>How Jordan uses it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>When weekly weight jumps stop working.</strong> Assuming the lifter is not overtrained and is recovered, his first steps are to slow the eccentric tempo, or pause the lift at the maximal lengthened position for 3 seconds and then push through. For the bench, that position is the bottom of the rep, on the chest.</li>
    <li><strong>To add volume.</strong> In his description of an 8 week progression, he says the volume can come from tempo too, which means slower reps count toward the work.</li>
    <li><strong>In a squat example.</strong> He uses sets such as 10 sets of 6 or 10 sets of 4 with a 4 to 5 second eccentric. That is a squat plan, not a bench plan, but it shows the scale he works at.</li>
    <li><strong>To find the problem.</strong> When a beginner stalls, he says it is usually form: movements that are not coordinated and smooth. A slow rep makes those flaws easier to see and feel.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the research says about speed and muscle growth</h2>
  <p>Schoenfeld and colleagues (2015) pooled eight studies that compared different rep tempos, each lasting at least 6 weeks with sets taken to muscle failure. Muscle growth was similar with repetition durations from 0.5 to 8 seconds. Training at volitionally very slow durations, longer than 10 seconds per rep, looked worse for growth, though the authors point out there were too few controlled studies to be definitive. For you: if your goal is a bigger chest, a normal, controlled tempo works. You do not need to crawl.</p>

  <h2>What we do not know</h2>
  <p>We did not find a controlled study that compares a paused bench with touch-and-go for strength gains. Coaches argue for pauses because meets require them and because they build control off the chest, and some say lifters are a few percent stronger touch-and-go, but those are coaching opinions, not measured results. One related finding from lab work on the lift: Gomo and van den Tillaar tested 12 experienced powerlifters and found a clear sticking region with every grip width, which means the bar slows somewhere on a max rep whichever grip is used. See <a href="/why-my-bench-press-is-weak/">why your bench is weak</a>.</p>

  <h2>How to use tempo on the bench</h2>
  <p>This section is general coaching practice, not a result from a study.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Drop the weight.</strong> Slow and paused reps are harder than normal ones, so use a lighter bar. Pick the weight that gives the effort you want, for example RPE 7, and use the <a href="/bench-press-tools/rpe-calculator/">RPE calculator</a> to plan it.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep the same bar path.</strong> The point is control, so the bar should travel the same way as in your normal reps.</li>
    <li><strong>An example block.</strong> 4 sets of 4 reps with a 3 second lowering and a 1 second pause on the chest, at a weight you could do 7 or 8 reps with, twice a week for 4 weeks, then go back to normal reps and compare.</li>
    <li><strong>Judge it by your log.</strong> If your normal-speed bench goes up afterward, keep it. If not, drop it.</li>
  </ul>
